searchData={"items":[{"type":"extras","title":"Overview","doc":"Med Fabric \n\n \n     \n     \n \n\nMed Fabric is a deployable Elixir reference exam that brings together two-way\nWebRTC talkback, live diagnostic audio capture with\n[MedEx](https://github.com/nshkrdotcom/med_ex), and clinician review of\nrecordings and pulse-rate measurements. PostgreSQL records acceptance and queues\nHL7 FHIR R4 delivery.","ref":"readme.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Run the exam candidate - Overview","doc":"The 0.2 candidate combines two-way conversation, independently selected diagnostic audio and clinician-entered pulse measurements. A patient can recover a recording outcome after reconnecting. Clinicians can seek in a verified WAV, review its source and time, and deliver the accepted observation to the encounter’s own FHIR destination.\n\nFrom this checkout, with MedEx checked out alongside it, run the SDK and Fabric QC sequence:\n\n```bash\nbash ops/qc_candidate.sh\n```\n\nThe command prepares the SDK's Weld release bundle, checks its browser assets,\nand runs the exam release against that exact bundle's `project/` directory.\nCommit SDK source changes first: Weld requires a clean source revision. The\ncandidate dependency lock stays under `apps/exam/_build/`. Allow time for native\ndependencies, Dialyzer, PostgreSQL, HAPI and Chromium. For a live exam, follow the\noperator setup. For an actual TURN demonstration use the two-endpoint media\nprocedure. [Browser-to-record guide](guides/browser_to_record.md) ·\n[Remote media verification](guides/media_verification.md) ·\n[Architecture and roadmap](guides/architecture_and_roadmap.md)","ref":"readme.html#run-the-exam-candidate"},{"type":"extras","title":"Projects - Overview","doc":"| Path | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- |\n| [core/domain](core/domain/README.md) | Validate audio and vital evidence constructors in pure Elixir |\n| [core/store](core/store/README.md) | Commit encounters, evidence, acceptance, and outbox intents atomically in PostgreSQL |\n| [core/interop](core/interop/README.md) | Map reviewed recordings and pulse rates to HL7 FHIR R4 and confirm idempotent delivery |\n| [apps/exam](apps/exam/README.md) | Boot Phoenix, authorize encounters, host talkback/preview/PCM sockets, and serve the review UI |","ref":"readme.html#projects"},{"type":"extras","title":"License - Overview","doc":"Released under the MIT License.","ref":"readme.html#license"},{"type":"extras","title":"Browser to record","doc":"# Browser to record\n\nThis guide covers Ubuntu 26.04 prerequisites, running the automated release\nverifier, building and running the compiled `mf_exam` Mix release directly, the\nmanual listening check, and optional local workspace SDK testing.","ref":"browser_to_record.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Prerequisites (Ubuntu 26.04) - Browser to record","doc":"1. **System packages:**\n\n   ```bash\n   sudo apt-get install -y git curl tar build-essential pkg-config libssl-dev jq openssl\n   ```\n\n   *(Install `libopus-dev` as well if compiling `membrane_opus_plugin` from\n   source instead of using precompiled binaries.)*\n\n2. **Pinned runtimes (`.tool-versions` via `asdf` or `mise`):**\n   - Erlang `29.1.2`\n   - Elixir `1.20.5-otp-29`\n   - Node.js `24.19.0` (required for `--integration` / Chromium verification)\n\n3. **Native services (or external URLs):**\n   - **PostgreSQL 18** server binaries (`initdb`, `pg_ctl`, `createdb`, `psql`),\n     or an existing test database via `DATABASE_URL`.\n   - **Temurin JDK `21.0.9+10`** (full JDK with `javac 21.0.9`) with `JAVA_HOME`\n     set when using the built-in local HAPI FHIR 7.6.0 runner, or an existing R4\n     FHIR server via `FHIR_BASE_URL`.","ref":"browser_to_record.html#prerequisites-ubuntu-26-04"},{"type":"extras","title":"Verify the unpublished candidate - Browser to record","doc":"Use sibling checkouts named `med_ex` and `med_fabric`. Commit MedEx changes\nbefore running: Weld prepares from a clean source revision. The script passes\nthe exact prepared release bundle's `project/` path to Mix and compares its\nbrowser assets with the tested sources. Candidate dependency resolution uses a\nseparate lockfile under `apps/exam/_build`.\n\n```bash\nbash ops/qc_candidate.sh\n```\n\nThis runs SDK QC and Weld preparation, the browser-helper unit tests, Fabric core tests and static analysis, docs, the exam dependency audit, native database and HAPI cases, recording recovery, and a compiled-release browser scenario. It uses the generated local SDK for the exam. Once MedEx 0.2 is on Hex, resolve it from `apps/exam` with `mix deps.get`, commit that lock, then use the ordinary `./onboard.sh --integration` and `bash ops/verify_browser.sh --release` workflow. The 0.1 Hex package implements an earlier browser protocol.\n\nUse a fresh dedicated test database and artifact directory for the candidate. The verifier creates synthetic clinical records. If `DATABASE_URL` and `FHIR_BASE_URL` are unset it owns isolated native services and restarts HAPI during the browser run. Providing those URLs targets your dedicated integration services.","ref":"browser_to_record.html#verify-the-unpublished-candidate"},{"type":"extras","title":"Operate the exam - Browser to record","doc":"From the Fabric root, create the dedicated volume, start the services and set the encounter identity in your shell:\n\n```bash\numask 077\nexport MF_ENCOUNTERS_FILE=\"$PWD/_build/encounters.json\"\nexport MF_ARTIFACT_DIR=\"$PWD/_build/capture-volume\"\nexport MF_SECRET_KEY_BASE=\"$(openssl rand -hex 48)\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$MF_ENCOUNTERS_FILE\")\" \"$MF_ARTIFACT_DIR\"\neval \"$(bash ops/local_services.sh start)\"\nexport MF_EXAM_ID=exam-a MF_PATIENT_ACTOR=patient-a MF_CLINICIAN_ACTOR=clinician-a\nexport MF_PATIENT_ACCESS_KEY=\"$(openssl rand -hex 24)\"\nexport MF_CLINICIAN_ACCESS_KEY=\"$(openssl rand -hex 24)\"\nexport MF_RELEASE_DIR=\"$PWD/_build/exam-release\"\n```\n\nFor development outside candidate QC, a disposable SDK projection can be used\nwith ordinary Mix as below. For release QC, use `ops/qc_candidate.sh` and the\nprepared project path it prints; this development projection is a separate tree.\n\n```bash\n(cd ../med_ex && mix weld.project)\nexport MF_MED_EX_PATH=\"$(cd ../med_ex/dist/monolith/med_ex && pwd)\"\n(\n  cd apps/exam\n  mkdir -p _build\n  cp mix.lock _build/candidate.mix.lock\n  mix deps.get\n  env -u MF_LAB mix run --no-start ../../ops/seed_exam.exs\n  env -u MF_LAB MIX_ENV=prod mix release --path \"$MF_RELEASE_DIR\" --overwrite\n)\nMF_LAB=1 \"$MF_RELEASE_DIR/bin/mf_exam\" start\n```\n\nFor the published SDK, leave `MF_MED_EX_PATH` unset and use the standard Mix lockfile.\n\nRun the seed command sequentially for each synthetic encounter, changing the ID, actors and keys. It writes a private JSON array. Each entry carries its own patient/clinician actors, FHIR destination, Patient reference and Encounter reference. Boot binds those identifiers in PostgreSQL. Conflicting existing bindings fail startup. The lab seed marks its FHIR encounter in progress. To use existing resources, provision the file directly. Keep the file outside Git.\n\nThe local endpoint listens on `127.0.0.1` at `MF_HTTP_PORT` (default 4000). In separate browser profiles open the encounter with the relevant key, for example `http://localhost:4000/encounters/exam-a?key=YOUR_PATIENT_KEY`. The server exchanges it for a session and redirects. Select conversation microphones independently on each side. On the patient page select the diagnostic input, start capture, and finish. If the reply is lost, reload the same tab or select **Check recording outcome**. The clinician sees recording duration, source label and first-frame receipt time, can seek during playback and explicitly accept and deliver either evidence kind. Stop the release with Ctrl-C and stop owned services with `bash ops/local_services.sh stop`.","ref":"browser_to_record.html#operate-the-exam"},{"type":"extras","title":"HTTPS and relay - Browser to record","doc":"Keep the application bound to loopback behind an authenticating reverse proxy. Set `MF_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the external HTTPS origin and provision a random `MF_PROXY_SECRET` of at least 32 bytes in the proxy and exam. Authenticate every HTTP and WebSocket upgrade at the proxy. Strip any client-supplied `X-Exam-Actor` and `X-Exam-Proxy-Token` headers and inject the authenticated actor ID and secret. The actor must match a configured encounter participant. HTTPS mode uses the proxy identity in place of lab URL keys. Restrict backend and proxy secret access to the trusted host. Configure secure cookies and TLS at the public ingress.\n\nSet `MF_TURN_URLS` to comma-separated TURN URIs and `MF_TURN_SECRET` to the shared coturn REST auth secret. The exam issues one-hour credentials when an authorized peer connects. Set `MF_RELAY_ONLY=1` for a forced-relay run. TURN transports the two WebRTC paths. The authoritative PCM stream uses a separate authenticated WebSocket. Use the remote media guide for two hosts, known-signal checks, connection and recovery times, and selected candidate-pair evidence. [Remote media verification](media_verification.md)","ref":"browser_to_record.html#https-and-relay"},{"type":"extras","title":"Targeted checks - Browser to record","doc":"- `core/domain` runs pure evidence cases with `mix test`.\n- `core/store` runs native transaction and encounter-isolation cases with `mix test integration` and `DATABASE_URL`.\n- `core/interop` runs pure FHIR comparison cases with `mix test`. Run `mix test integration` with `FHIR_BASE_URL` for the native HAPI retry.\n- `apps/exam` runs artifact, range and proxy cases with `mix test`, and the interrupted recording scenarios with `env -u MF_LAB mix test integration` and `DATABASE_URL`. Set `MF_MED_EX_PATH` for the unpublished SDK.\n- The deployable exam currently pins the single dependency-constraint change from Ratio PR 131 at commit `05191db`. The lock resolves Numbers 5.2.5 and Decimal 3.2.0. Run `mix hex.audit` in the exam and review the pinned Git change separately. Remove the Git pin when a Hex Ratio release admits Decimal 3. MedEx itself continues to declare Hex dependencies. [Ratio PR 131](https://github.com/qqwy/elixir-rational/pull/131)\n\nSee [Architecture and roadmap](architecture_and_roadmap.md) for storage ownership and clinical invariants.","ref":"browser_to_record.html#targeted-checks"},{"type":"extras","title":"Browser media verification","doc":"# Browser media verification\n\nThe browser capture checkpoint sends a generated 997 Hz mono microphone signal through Chromium's capture device. The patient sends indexed PCM to the exam and the clinician receives decoded Opus over WebRTC. Use a fresh synthetic encounter for each capture run. The checkpoint creates one recording and one manual pulse.","ref":"media_verification.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Local run - Browser media verification","doc":"For the unpublished MedEx candidate, run the following command from the Fabric checkout. It resolves the sibling SDK, builds the exam release and runs the browser checkpoints. [Prerequisites and workspace setup](browser_to_record.md)\n\n```sh\nbash ops/qc_candidate.sh\n```\n\nThe wrapper controls the local PostgreSQL, HAPI and exam processes across three checkpoints. It prints the capture measurements as JSON, restarts the exam, accepts and exports both records, then checks remote identities and the WAV after restart. Both role contexts in the local scenario share one Chromium process.","ref":"media_verification.html#local-run"},{"type":"extras","title":"Relay run with separate network endpoints - Browser media verification","doc":"Provision a patient machine and a clinician machine on different networks. Both must trust the exam's HTTPS certificate and reach the configured TURN server and its UDP relay port range. The server-side preview peer also needs outbound connectivity to coturn. Synchronize coturn and exam server clocks for REST credential expiry.\n\nRun real coturn with REST authentication (`use-auth-secret`, `static-auth-secret` and a configured realm), a listener and relay addresses reachable by all peers. When coturn is behind NAT, map the advertised external address and relay port range to the relay interface. On the exam host export `MF_RELAY_ONLY=1`, `MF_TURN_URLS=turn:turn.example.test:3478?transport=udp`, `MF_TURN_SECRET` matching coturn's REST secret, and `MF_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://exam.example.test`. Use a dedicated synthetic encounter in `MF_ENCOUNTERS_FILE`. Put the authenticating HTTPS proxy ahead of the loopback Phoenix endpoint, including its WebSocket upgrade routes. The proxy strips incoming identity headers and supplies the authenticated actor and `MF_PROXY_SECRET` to the exam.\n\nOn each browser machine, install the checkout's pinned Playwright and Chromium. Launch one loopback endpoint on each machine.\n\n```sh\ncd ops/browser\nnpm ci\nnpx playwright install --with-deps chromium\nMF_BROWSER_PORT=9333 node endpoint.mjs\n```\n\nEach launcher prints a private WebSocket endpoint with an unguessable path. Keep its terminal open. Use two SSH forwards from the control machine and retain each endpoint's path.\n\n```sh\nssh -NT -L 19333:127.0.0.1:9333 patient-browser\n# In a second terminal.\nssh -NT -L 29333:127.0.0.1:9333 clinician-browser\n```\n\nOn the control machine, install the same pinned Playwright version. Create two role-specific Playwright storage-state files using the deployment's identity provider. For a cookie-based browser login, run the commands below in separate fresh sessions, complete the login as the indicated actor and close the browser to save its state. Keep the state files and endpoint paths private. The proxy must recognize each role session when used from its browser machine. Use deployment-provisioned role states if the identity provider binds sessions to the originating device.\n\n```sh\ncd ops/browser\nnpm ci\nnpx playwright install chromium\numask 077\nmkdir -p \"$HOME/.local/state/med-fabric-qc\"\nexport MF_EXAM_ID=relay-trial-1\nexport MF_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://exam.example.test\nexport MF_PATIENT_STORAGE_STATE=\"$HOME/.local/state/med-fabric-qc/patient.json\"\nexport MF_CLINICIAN_STORAGE_STATE=\"$HOME/.local/state/med-fabric-qc/clinician.json\"\nnpx playwright codegen --save-storage=\"$MF_PATIENT_STORAGE_STATE\" \"$MF_PUBLIC_ORIGIN/encounters/$MF_EXAM_ID\"\nnpx playwright codegen --save-storage=\"$MF_CLINICIAN_STORAGE_STATE\" \"$MF_PUBLIC_ORIGIN/encounters/$MF_EXAM_ID\"\n\nexport MF_PATIENT_BROWSER_WS='ws://127.0.0.1:19333/ '\nexport MF_CLINICIAN_BROWSER_WS='ws://127.0.0.1:29333/ '\nset -o pipefail\nnode check.mjs --capture --relay | tee \"$HOME/.local/state/med-fabric-qc/relay.json\"\n```\n\nFor the export checkpoints, set `FHIR_BASE_URL` on the control machine to the synthetic HAPI endpoint used by this encounter. Restart the exam on its host between checkpoints. The control machine needs read access to that FHIR endpoint. Refresh the browser endpoint variables if the endpoint processes restart.\n\n```sh\nnode check.mjs --relay\n# Restart the exam and HAPI with their persisted volumes, then rerun the read-back.\nnode check.mjs --relay --after-restart\n```\n\nThe Chromium processes run on the endpoint machines. SSH carries Playwright control traffic. ICE, RTP, PCM, and authenticated page traffic originate at the browsers. The API requests that inspect authorized HTTP byte ranges originate at the Playwright API context, which carries the role's session cookies. Record endpoint networks, coturn version, exam/SDK revisions, and deployment configuration alongside the JSON for each run. Keep credentials out of the report.","ref":"media_verification.html#relay-run-with-separate-network-endpoints"},{"type":"extras","title":"Interpreting the measurements - Browser media verification","doc":"- **ICE and RTP.** The harness reads the transport's selected candidate-pair ID and checks for a succeeded pair. Relay mode requires both selected candidates to be `relay` for patient and clinician talkback and for the clinician-to-server preview. It reports transport protocol, round-trip time when exposed, received/lost packet counts, jitter in seconds and decoded sample count. Measurements include replacement and rejoin.\n- **Setup and interruption.** Connect-to-state and join-to-media times are measured from the controller. The observing clinician also samples inbound audio counters on its own monotonic clock about every 50 ms. The reported gap spans the last observed increase on the displaced peer and the first on the replacement. Preview replacement includes the period listening from a second clinician tab. Sampling cadence, stats publication and control round trips affect these intervals.\n- **Signal.** Decoded preview must exceed RMS 0.01 on a normalized scale and have an FFT peak within 30 Hz of 997 Hz, both before and after replacement. The finalized WAV must contain a 100 ms window whose rising zero crossings and RMS agree with the microphone tone. This fixture exercises the browser path. Named physical inputs and calibration require separate device evidence.\n- **Lossless recording.** The harness hashes outgoing browser WebSocket PCM in order, checks every starting index, observes finish, then requires an exact SHA-256 and sample count match for the WAV data. The independent file digest is checked against the ETag and exported FHIR value. The SDK's `peakBufferedBytes` and `maxAckDelayMs` describe the PCM connection, including preview encoder startup while recording. The capture workload must fit the 32,776-byte logical bound.\n- **Authorized seeking.** Concurrent clinician requests check exact 206 bodies and Content-Range for bounded and suffix ranges. A past-end request must return 416 and the total size. A patient request for the same byte range must return 404.","ref":"media_verification.html#interpreting-the-measurements"},{"type":"extras","title":"Architecture and roadmap","doc":"# Architecture and roadmap\n\nMed Fabric owns clinical encounters, authorization, review, durable observations,\nand healthcare record export. `apps/exam` (`mf_exam`) is the composition root: it\ndepends on [MedEx](https://github.com/nshkrdotcom/med_ex) for live media capture\nand diagnostic preview, PostgreSQL for authoritative clinical state, and an\nexternal HL7 FHIR R4 server for delivery. The three core clinical libraries\n(`core/domain`, `core/store`, `core/interop`) remain independent of `apps/exam`.","ref":"architecture_and_roadmap.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Exam composition - Architecture and roadmap","doc":"Each authorized encounter composes three independent paths:\n\n1. **Two-way conversation:** Direct browser-to-browser WebRTC audio talkback\n   between patient and clinician, authorized and signaled by `apps/exam`.\n2. **Live diagnostic audio capture:** Patient browser `AudioWorklet` streams\n   indexed 48 kHz mono PCM16LE over an authenticated binary WebSocket to\n   `PCMSocket`, which feeds `MedEx.Capture` (immutable WAV writer + bounded\n   live `Membrane.Opus.Encoder` preview over a replaceable `MedEx.Preview` peer).\n3. **Fileless vital measurement:** Clinician-entered pulse rate with timestamp\n   and explicit manual provenance—zero WAV file dependency.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Patient <-->|WebRTC conversation| Clinician\n  Patient -->|Binary PCM WebSocket| Exam\n  Exam -->|Indexed PCM| MedEx\n  MedEx -->|Replaceable Opus preview| Clinician\n  MedEx -->|Finalized WAV evidence| Exam\n  Vital -->|Manual pulse rate| Exam\n  Exam -->|Explicit acceptance| Store[(PostgreSQL)]\n  Store -->|Committed outbox| FHIR\n```\n\nAn encounter outlives its peer connections. A diagnostic acquisition owns its\ncapture lifetime. A preview listener can disconnect independently. Loss of the patient producer ends an unfinished recording.\nAfter evidence is finalized and stored, PostgreSQL owns review, acceptance, and\nexport status across application restarts. Acceptance and its FHIR delivery\nintent commit in one SQL transaction. FHIR HTTP runs outside that transaction\nand outside LiveView.","ref":"architecture_and_roadmap.html#exam-composition"},{"type":"extras","title":"State and effects review - Architecture and roadmap","doc":"| Owner | State and reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| `MedEx.Capture` | Temporary `.part` WAV writer, next expected sample index, running PCM SHA-256, and single-frame preview demand. Enforces byte order, detects source loss, and keeps preview bounded. |\n| `PCMSocket` and the temporary writer | A reserved attempt binds the acquisition ID and patient before PCM. Per-encounter admission serializes writer creation, finish and recovery. The live writer owns the name used by preview discovery. |\n| `SignalingChannel` | One `MedEx.Preview` peer and its RTP sequence and clock state. `MedEx.Preview.Clock` owns media-clock arithmetic and `send_opus/4` owns RTP wrapping. A replacement peer starts a fresh transport sequence. |\n| PostgreSQL (`core/store`) | Encounter membership, typed evidence + provenance, accepted observations, and FHIR outbox intents. Row locks serialize review and acceptance. The exam verifies immutable WAV bytes before entering the acceptance transaction. |\n| LiveView & browser | Presentation state, user actions, and ephemeral media handles. FHIR HTTP runs in an async delivery action; PostgreSQL owns the delivery outcome. |\n\nThe deployment supports one BEAM instance with its own persistent artifact volume and database. It can host multiple encounters concurrently. An authenticated reverse proxy supplies identity for HTTPS deployments. The exam enforces membership, destination and session scope. Localhost has a separate role-key entry flow.\n\n`MedFabric.Exam.member?/4` centralizes patient/clinician membership checks used\nat transport entry. Exam workflows accept an optional `Exam.Context` carrying\nthe concrete Postgrex connection, capture supervisor, PubSub name, artifact\ndirectory, and configured encounter IDs. Production defaults resolve at service\nentry; integration tests supply isolated resources without replacing application\nconfiguration. The context excludes ingress credentials and TURN secrets.\n\nAdmission, finish, recovery, and status reconciliation retain the per-encounter\n`:global.trans` lock on the local node. The database reservation constraint\ncontinues to enforce one recording per encounter. Acceptance still rechecks\nclinical authorization and evidence inside its transaction.","ref":"architecture_and_roadmap.html#state-and-effects-review"},{"type":"extras","title":"PostgreSQL schema and invariants - Architecture and roadmap","doc":"The store applies the scripts under `core/store/priv/sql/` in filename order under a transaction lock.\n\n| Table | Purpose & key invariants |\n| --- | --- |\n| `encounters` | Encounter ID (`PK`), `patient_actor`, `clinician_actor`, and nullable `closed_at`. |\n| `acquisitions` | `(encounter_id, id)` `PK`, `evidence` (`jsonb`), and `conflict` flag. Recording reservations also store producer, first accepted frame receipt time and source settings until finalization. A partial unique index allows one recording in progress per encounter. Ready evidence constraints enforce required `kind` and `provenance` object, requiring `artifact_key` + `file_sha256` for `audio_pcm16_wav` and forbidding artifact fields while requiring numeric `value` for `pulse_rate`. |\n| `encounter_exports` | The immutable FHIR destination and subject/encounter references for one encounter. |\n| `observations` | `(encounter_id, acquisition_id)` `PK` referencing `acquisitions`, `actor`, and `accepted_at`. Guarantees at most one accepted observation per acquisition; late conflicts cannot overwrite accepted records. |\n| `outbox_deliveries` | `(encounter_id, acquisition_id)` `PK` referencing `observations`, `destination`, `external_id`, `resource` (`jsonb`), `remote_ref`, and `last_error`. Enforces `UNIQUE (destination, external_id)` for idempotent FHIR retries. |\n\n`core/store` performs PostgreSQL transactions only—it performs no filesystem\n`File.read/1` calls and no HTTP calls.\n\nThe socket reports an acquisition ID before the browser sends audio. A finalized canonical WAV lives at ` / / .wav`. On startup, next admission or an outcome lookup, recovery validates bytes and restores reviewable evidence or marks the abandoned partial interrupted. A live writer retains ownership. Acceptance checks the named evidence off the LiveView process. Authorized playback verifies and streams the same descriptor in bounded chunks with byte ranges. Use a fresh database and volume for this greenfield release. Retain any 0.1 artifacts with their corresponding release.\n\nArtifact verification uses `MedEx.Wav.parse_header/2` and retains both complete\nWAV and PCM SHA-256 checks, including every playback request. Verified file\nmetadata alone carries no continuity claim. The locked recovery coordinator\ncombines it with a reserved attempt and the contract that only Capture installs\nfinalized WAVs in its managed directory. Range and If-Range handling remain in\nthe HTTP artifact layer. The first PCM acknowledgement still waits for the\ndatabase's first-frame receipt update as well as the writer call.","ref":"architecture_and_roadmap.html#postgresql-schema-and-invariants"},{"type":"extras","title":"HL7 FHIR R4 mapping and confirmation - Architecture and roadmap","doc":"`MedFabric.Interop.FhirClient` maps accepted clinical observations to FHIR R4\n`Observation` resources and delivers them via conditional create\n(`POST /Observation` with `If-None-Exist: identifier=urn:med-fabric:observation| `\nand `Prefer: return=representation`), followed by strict typed field read-back\nconfirmation (rejecting unexpected clinical modifiers or conflicting values):\n\n| Evidence `kind` | FHIR `code` | FHIR value & `method` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `pulse_rate` | LOINC `8867-4` (`http://loinc.org`) | `valueQuantity` in UCUM `/min` (`beats/minute`), method `\"Manually entered measurement\"`. |\n| `audio_pcm16_wav` | `reviewed-auscultation-recording` (`https://med-fabric.local/fhir/CodeSystem/exam-observation`) | `valueString` set to `file_sha256`, method `\"Reviewed diagnostic audio recording\"`. |\n\nThe pulse comparison includes the FHIR quantity comparator. An exact value and a bound such as “less than 70” cannot confirm each other. The two supported Observation shapes permit server envelope metadata, reference display enrichment and a coding display while protecting the submitted clinical fields. Outbox delivery remains clinician-triggered and safely retryable.","ref":"architecture_and_roadmap.html#hl7-fhir-r4-mapping-and-confirmation"},{"type":"extras","title":"Capability families and 12-use-case roadmap - Architecture and roadmap","doc":"| Family | Shared requirements | Target use cases | Current implementation and roadmap |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Clinical collaboration | Session roles, real-time media, annotations, escalation | Connected telehealth exam (3), ultrasound & imaging (6), tele-EMS (7), speech therapy (9), procedural guidance (12) | **Implemented:** Patient–clinician 1:1 WebRTC audio talkback + role separation. **Planned:** Video tracks, multiparty SFU, annotations, additional identity providers and team roles. |\n| Device acquisition & monitoring | Source identity, timing, calibration, typed evidence | Digital stethoscope (1), RPM (4), respiratory devices (8), device troubleshooting (10), hearing/assistive devices (11) | **Implemented:** Browser 48 kHz mono PCM16LE audio + manual pulse-rate evidence in one encounter. **Planned:** Hardware USB/BLE device gateways, multi-channel ECG/SpO₂ telemetry, calibration & control channels. |\n| Clinical audio & signal processing | Signal fidelity, timing, lossless recording, quality checks | Digital stethoscope (1), voice monitoring (2), respiratory devices (8), speech therapy (9), hearing/assistive devices (11) | **Implemented:** Bit-exact lossless WAV capture independent of live Opus preview + peer replacement recovery. **Planned:** Bell/diaphragm filtering, clipping/SNR QC, waveform review UI, out-of-band signal workers. |\n| AI-assisted workflows | Consent, model provenance, explicit clinician review, escalation | Voice monitoring (2), medical voice assistants (5) | **Implemented:** Explicit clinician acceptance + persisted provenance structure. **Planned:** Derived-result provenance schema constraints, ASR/TTS/VAD voice pipelines, model workers, escalation rules. |","ref":"architecture_and_roadmap.html#capability-families-and-12-use-case-roadmap"},{"type":"extras","title":"Extension boundaries - Architecture and roadmap","doc":"[MedEx](https://github.com/nshkrdotcom/med_ex/blob/main/guides/architecture_and_roadmap.md)\nowns live media capture and preview transport. Out-of-band waveform processing\nand inference (such as future NeuroKit2 or WFDB-Python workers, ONNX/Ortex\nmodels, or Pipecat/whisper.cpp voice pipelines) are **future work**;\nwhen built, they will run as separately deployed workers or opt-in packages\nconsuming finalized artifacts outside capture and submitting derived results\nwith source digest and algorithm provenance into Fabric's review workflow.\nLiveKit fits at the multiparty SFU boundary when needed; Orthanc fits at the\nDICOM imaging/export boundary.\n\nThe exam deploys as an OTP Mix release. Keeping `core/domain`,\n`core/store`, and `core/interop` decoupled from `apps/exam` preserves the option\nto package and publish a reusable `:med_fabric` Hex library later once a second\napplication defines its public library API.\n\nSee [Browser to record](browser_to_record.md) for prerequisites, release\ndeployment, verification, and the manual listening check.","ref":"architecture_and_roadmap.html#extension-boundaries"},{"type":"extras","title":"Domain","doc":"Pure encounter decisions \n\n \n     \n     \n \n\n`mf_domain` (`core/domain`) provides pure Elixir clinical evidence constructors\nfor [Med Fabric](../../README.md), with zero database, Phoenix, or HTTP\ndependencies.\n\n---","ref":"domain.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Domain rules - Domain","doc":"`MedFabric.Domain.Evidence` validates finalized `audio_pcm16_wav` recording\nevidence (48 kHz mono digests, sample count, continuity, and browser capture\nprovenance) and operator-entered `pulse_rate` measurements (positive integer\nbeats/minute with explicit manual provenance). PostgreSQL (`core/store`) owns\nconcurrent review and acceptance state.\n\nRun `mix test` here, or see the [browser-to-record guide](../../guides/browser_to_record.md).","ref":"domain.html#domain-rules"},{"type":"extras","title":"Store","doc":"Clinical persistence and audit \n\n \n     \n     \n \n\n`mf_store` (`core/store`) owns atomic persistence of encounters, typed evidence,\nclinician-accepted observations, and transactional FHIR export outbox intents in\n[Med Fabric](../../README.md).\n\n---","ref":"store.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Persistence - Store","doc":"`MedFabric.Store.Postgres` runs the SQL files in `priv/sql/` in filename order\nunder an advisory transaction lock. Use a dedicated candidate database and\nvolume. Each recording has an acquisition-specific directory on that volume.\nKeep both stores across exam restarts. Existing 0.1.0 recordings retain\ntheir original flat file layout and database records in the 0.1.0 deployment.\n\nAdmission reserves an encounter, acquisition ID and producer before the writer\nreceives PCM. The first accepted frame persists the browser source settings\nand server receipt time. Only a ready acquisition appears in the review list.\nThe acceptance transaction compares the exact reviewed evidence and writes\nthe observation and delivery intent together.\n\nOne running exam instance owns the configured encounters and local recording\nvolume. Startup, status lookup and the next admission can reconcile an\nabandoned recording. A validated final WAV becomes ready. An attempt with\nonly an unfinished file becomes interrupted and the writer's temporary file\nis removed. A filesystem or database I/O error leaves the attempt available\nfor another recovery pass.\n\nWith `DATABASE_URL` set, the store's `mix test integration` exercises\ntransaction rollback and concurrent acceptance. From `apps/exam`, use\n`mix test integration/recording_test.exs` against a dedicated test database\nwith `MF_LAB` unset to exercise recording interruptions and near-limit range\nplayback. [Browser-to-record guide](../../guides/browser_to_record.md)","ref":"store.html#persistence"},{"type":"extras","title":"Interop","doc":"Healthcare record exchange \n\n \n     \n     \n \n\n`mf_interop` (`core/interop`) is the single authoritative healthcare record\nexchange library for [Med Fabric](../../README.md) (kept outside\n[MedEx](https://github.com/nshkrdotcom/med_ex)). It maps accepted observations\ninto HL7 FHIR R4 `Observation` resources and delivers them idempotently over\nHTTP using `Req`.\n\n---","ref":"interop.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Delivery - Interop","doc":"`MedFabric.Interop.FhirClient` maps reviewed recording digests\n(`reviewed-auscultation-recording`) and manually entered pulse rates (LOINC\n`8867-4`, UCUM `/min`) into distinct FHIR R4 `Observation` payloads. It uses\nconditional create (`If-None-Exist`) and confirms remote identity and clinical\nvalues on read-back, including the presence or absence of a quantity comparator.\n\nUse `FHIR_BASE_URL` and `mix test integration` to exercise retry against native\nHAPI. See the [browser-to-record guide](../../guides/browser_to_record.md).","ref":"interop.html#delivery"},{"type":"extras","title":"Exam","doc":"Connected remote examination \n\n \n     \n     \n \n\n`mf_exam` (`apps/exam`) is the deployable Phoenix + LiveView reference\nexamination application in [Med Fabric](../../README.md). It consumes the single\nWelded [`:med_ex`](https://github.com/nshkrdotcom/med_ex) SDK and composes\n`mf_domain`, `mf_store`, and `mf_interop`.\n\n---","ref":"exam.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Browser-to-record path - Exam","doc":"The Phoenix app starts its own endpoint, PubSub, PostgreSQL connection, schema\nmigration, and capture supervisor when `MF_LAB=1` is configured. Patient and\nclinician browsers negotiate a direct two-way WebRTC talkback call. An\nindependent authorized channel negotiates the clinician's receive-only\ndiagnostic preview peer while the patient browser streams live 48 kHz mono\nPCM16LE over the encounter-scoped recording WebSocket. MedEx finalizes the lossless WAV independently of preview,\nand the clinician reviews that recording alongside manually entered pulse-rate\nmeasurements. PostgreSQL commits acceptance and export intent atomically, and an\nasync delivery action confirms each `Observation` against the FHIR R4 server.\n\nSee the [browser-to-record guide](../../guides/browser_to_record.md) for\nprerequisites, Mix release startup, automated verification, and the listening\ncheck.","ref":"exam.html#browser-to-record-path"},{"type":"extras","title":"Changelog","doc":"# Med Fabric changelog","ref":"changelog.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"0.2.0 (unreleased) - Changelog","doc":"- Centralize transport membership checks and pass explicit workflow resources\n  through recording and review services, including isolated integration tests.\n- Reuse the SDK's WAV validation and preview clock rules; retain full-file\n  integrity verification and same-descriptor range streaming.\n- Keep recovered file identity separate from the reservation-backed continuity\n  assertion in the recording coordinator.\n- Run combined candidate QC against the exact prepared SDK release project and\n  verify its packaged browser assets.\n- Reserve acquisitions before PCM, recover finalized evidence after interruption, and serve verified, seekable recordings with bounded memory.\n- Support encounter-scoped FHIR bindings, strict observation confirmation, distinct input devices, authenticated HTTPS ingress and TURN credentials.\n- Use the local MedEx 0.2 candidate until Hex publication. See the browser-to-record guide for the QC and release workflow.","ref":"changelog.html#0-2-0-unreleased"},{"type":"extras","title":"0.1.0 - Changelog","doc":"- Compose two-way WebRTC talkback, independent diagnostic preview and recording, and manual pulse review in one exam.\n- Commit clinical acceptance and its FHIR outbox intent atomically in PostgreSQL.\n- Verify the Mix release with Chromium and native PostgreSQL and HAPI.","ref":"changelog.html#0-1-0"},{"type":"extras","title":"License","doc":"MIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2026 nshkrdotcom\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\nin the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\nto use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\ncopies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\nfurnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\ncopies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\nIMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\nFITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\nAUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\nLIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\nOUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\nSOFTWARE.","ref":"license.html"}],"proglang":"elixir","content_type":"text/markdown","producer":{"name":"ex_doc","version":"0.40.5"}}